#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# semidefinite_optimization

#% text
# `semidefinite_optimization(f, cons, vars)` gives the point that minimizes `f` under constraints that may require a symmetric matrix of the variables to be positive semidefinite.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A single symbol as the variable is a vector variable whose length the dot products with it fix (`dot([a1, a2], v)` is `v1 a1 + v2 a2` for symmetric matrices).

#% item
# `vector_greater_equal([m, 0], [semidefinite_cone, n])` asks the matrix `m` to be positive semidefinite, that is all its principal minors to be non-negative.

#% item
# `semidefinite_optimization(c, [a0, a1, ..., an])` minimizes `c . x` such that `a0 + x1 a1 + ... + xn an` is positive semidefinite and returns the vector `x`.

#% item
# `semidefinite_optimization(c, [a0, ..., an], doms)` with `doms` `integers`, `reals` or a list of them asks the entries in `integers` to be integers (branch and bound, as for `linear_optimization`).

#% item
# A fourth argument selects the result as for `quadratic_optimization`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
semidefinite_optimization(x + y, vector_greater_equal([[[x, 1], [1, y]], 0], [semidefinite_cone, 2]), [x, y])
#% output
#= [x -> 1, y -> 1]

#% cell
semidefinite_optimization([2, 3], [[[0, 1], [1, 0]], [[1, 0], [0, 0]], [[0, 0], [0, 1]]])
#% output
#= [(1/2)*6^(1/2), (1/3)*6^(1/2)]

#% cell
semidefinite_optimization(x + 2*y, [x^2 + 2*y^2 <= 3, x + y == 2, x >= 1], [x, y])
#% output
#= [x -> 5/3, y -> 1/3]

#% cell
semidefinite_optimization(dot([1, 2], v), vector_greater_equal([dot([[[1, 0], [0, 0]], [[0, 0], [0, 1]]], v), -[[0, 1], [1, 0]]], [semidefinite_cone, 2]), v)
#% output
#= [v -> [2^(1/2), (1/2)*2^(1/2)]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
